        "original_id": "96f0aa10-8e93-5280-a3cf-ba5fb1437e44",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Projects: Interbol\n- Organizations: Russian Space Agency, Space Research Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences\n- Concepts: Plasma, states of matter\n- People: Irving Langmuir\n- Topics: Plasma physics\n\nFacts:\n- In the 1920s, chemist Irving Langmuir first introduced plasma as a concept.\n- Interbol is an international space project led by the Russian Space Agency and the Space Research Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences.\n- Plasma comes from the Ancient Greek word πλάσμα, meaning \"moldable substance\" or \"jelly.\"\n- Plasma can be described as a gaseous mixture of negatively charged electrons and highly charged positive ions.\n- True plasma production results from the distinct separation of ions and electrons, which creates an electric field that in turn produces electric currents and magnetic fields.\n- Plasma is one of the four fundamental states of matter.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "00d51d54-d9a2-5018-90b2-5a5f327a1e17",
        "text": "Page: Interbol. Sentence: Interbol (Russian: Интербол ) is an international space project under the leadership of the Russian Space Agency and the Space Research Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences.\nPage: Plasma (physics). Sentence: Plasma (from Ancient Greek πλάσμα , meaning \"moldable substance\" or \"jelly\") can simply be considered as a gaseous mixture of negatively charged electrons and highly charged positive ions, however, true plasma production is from the distinct separation of these ions and electrons that produces an electric field, which in turn, produces electric currents and magnetic fields. Plasma is one of the four fundamental states of matter and was first introduced by chemist Irving Langmuir in the 1920s.",
